From 5aed6276de1b68650648b89455e1e518bc34c2eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Christian F.K. Schaller" <christian.schaller@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 11:21:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] scanner: Add OpenScanner and sessions that hold a scanner
 open

This commit adds OpenScanner, which turns a granted scanner into a
Session. The helper gets a second mode, session NAME, in which it opens
the scanner, says whether that worked, and then serves requests from the
portal on file descriptor 3 until the portal closes the socket, when it
cancels and closes the scanner and exits. The two sides talk in
GVariants framed by their size, defined in scanner-protocol.h, which
sends with MSG_NOSIGNAL so that a helper that has gone away is an error
and not a SIGPIPE that would end the portal.

OpenScanner checks that the id was granted to the application, finds
the scanner's current SANE name (a USB scanner that was plugged in again
has a new one), and takes the host's exclusive lock before it starts the
helper, so that two applications asking at once cannot both open it. A
scanner that is open fails with the new Busy error, one that is not
granted with NotAllowed. Closing the session, the application leaving
the bus, or the helper exiting on its own releases the lock; in the last
case the application is sent Session.Closed. A helper that does not exit
within five seconds of its socket closing is killed.

Busy is a new error, org.freedesktop.portal.Error.Busy. A locked
scanner is an ordinary condition that an application handles
differently from a failure.

Sessions need a handle token, which other portals take from a
session_handle_token option. It is optional here and defaults to the
one that requests default to, with the portal making the handle unique
if it is taken, so an application that does not care gets a working
handle.

Tested through the frontend and the helper on a private bus with SANE's
test backend: another connection cannot open a granted scanner, an open
starts one helper, a second open is Busy, an unknown id is NotAllowed,
Session.Close ends the helper and releases the lock, a reopen works, and
the helper exits when the application leaves the bus. The helper was
also tested over a socketpair on its own: open, close, an unknown method,
the socket closing, and a device that is not there. Not tested: a helper
that crashes during a session, the forced kill, and two simultaneous
opens.
